data.content:
This is a **C# 14 span overload resolution breaking change**.

## What happened

In C# 14 / .NET 10, new built-in span conversions mean `array.Contains(num)` now resolves to **`MemoryExtensions.Contains`** (a `Span`-based method) instead of **`Enumerable.Contains`**. `MemoryExtensions.Contains` is **not representable in LINQ expression trees**, so the expression tree compilation throws at runtime.

## How to fix

Force the call to bind to `Enumerable.Contains` by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);

// Option 3: Cast to IEnumerable<T>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);
```

Any of these prevent the compiler from picking the span overload and keep the expression tree valid.
